<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-serine biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="SERSYN-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: SER| biosynthesis is a major metabolic pathway in many organisms. Its end product, |FRAME: SER|, is not only used in protein synthesis, but also as a precursor for the biosynthesis of glycine, cysteine, tryptophan, and phospholipids. In addition, it directly or indirectly serves as a source of one-carbon units for the biosynthesis of various compounds. In the pathway described here, which operates in archaea |CITS: [17071763]|, bacteria |CITS: [11354602]|, yeast, some protists, plant plastids and animals, serine is derived from |FRAME: G3P|, an intermediate of glycolysis. Regulation of the pathway is mainly accomplished by feedback inhibition of the enzyme that catalyzes the first committed step, |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.95|. The second enzyme in the pathway, |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.52|, requires |FRAME: PYRIDOXAL_PHOSPHATE| (PLP) as a cofactor and is also required for the biosynthesis of PLP itself. Thus the cell must ensure that the supply of PLP is adequate. Little biochemical work has been done on the final enzyme of the pathway, |FRAME: EC-3.1.3.3|. &lt;b&gt;Serine Biosynthesis in Plants&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: SER| biosynthesis in plants proceeds by three pathways. The pathway described here, which is called the phosphorylated pathway, operates in plastids. The phosphorylated pathway is thought to contribute to serine biosynthesis mostly in the dark or in non-photosynthetic tissues. Since the genes of the phosphorylated pathway are upregulated by high salinity, flood and cold, it is thought that the phosphorylated pathway is also important in supplying serine under environmental stresses. A second pathway, which has not been studied much, operates in the cytosol and is described in |FRAME: PWY-8011|. However, the best studied and arguably the most important serine biosynthesis pathway in plants, which is called the glycolate pathway, is part of the |FRAME: PWY-181 photorespiration| pathway. In that pathway |FRAME: SER| is formed |from glycolate via glycine. The glycolate pathway dominates in the photosynthetic tissues. Most of the serine produced in the phosphorylated pathway is fed into with the photorespiration pathway as input for |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.45|, and its carbon is directed to the |FRAME: CALVIN-PWY &quot;Calvin cycle&quot;| via |FRAME: OH-PYR| and |FRAME: GLYCERATE|.</Description>
